1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,940
These robots were trained
to jump into molten steel.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,480
Their own manufacturer
taught them to do it.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,200
It's not a special effect.

4
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:12,600
Figure, the humanoid robot company, says
the jumps were real, and the Finnish steel

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:15,740
plant that hosted them says its
spokesperson watched them go in.

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:19,140
So why turn expensive
machines into a spectacle?

7
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:21,760
And how do you teach a
robot its final jump?

8
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:27,660
Start with the why. These are F.02s,
Figure's second-generation humanoid.

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,540
Figure says they spent months
loading parts at a BMW plant.

10
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:37,160
But as its newer F.03 fleet grew, keeping
the old ones running stopped making sense.

11
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:39,860
So what do you do with a retired robot?

12
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,980
Sell
it? Figure says they're packed with its own custom hardware, like the actuators

13
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:47,860
that move the joints. It didn't
want that leaving the building.

14
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:53,120
Take it apart? Figure says doing that for
every robot would eat so much engineering

15
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,960
time, it would delay the next model.

16
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:56,940
Melt it? Simple.

17
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:00,460
Except a robot that jumps in on
its own has to bring its battery.

18
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:02,680
Figure says foundries in the U.S.

19
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,980
and Mexico refused.

20
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,680
One in Imatra, Finland, said yes.

21
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,300
Plenty of companies just
recycle old hardware.

22
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,280
But only one of these options
came with a film crew.

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,380
And the film? Blame a reply on X.

24
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:20,920
In August, Figure's CEO, Brett Adcock,
asked what to do with the old robots.

25
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:24,120
Arnold Schwarzenegger replied,
"You should melt them."

26
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:29,800
In Terminator 2, his robot character ends
up lowered into molten steel so no one can

27
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:33,400
copy its technology, which is
almost exactly Figure's reason.

28
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:36,540
Hollywood wrote the business case in 1991.

29
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,320
Figure took the hint, and Schwarzenegger
appears in the finished film.

30
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:45,270
Now the hard part. You can't rehearse
a jump like this in a real furnace.

31
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:49,130
So Figure says it trained a
new AI model in simulation.

32
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:51,730
Here's how that kind of
training generally works.

33
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:54,470
Thousands of virtual robots try the jump.

34
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:57,510
Each attempt gets a score.
Did it stay balanced?

35
00:01:57,850 --> 00:01:59,750
Push off cleanly? Land on target?

36
00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:04,190
The software nudges the controls toward
whatever scored well, and they try again,

37
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:08,130
millions of times. Virtual
robots are great at this.

38
00:02:08,510 --> 00:02:12,270
They can fall on their faces all day,
and nobody files an incident report.

39
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:16,970
Figure says it used stunt performers'
movements as a reference, a kind of style

40
00:02:17,090 --> 00:02:22,170
guide
for a good leap. The result is called a policy, a learned rule that turns what

41
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:25,610
the robot senses into motor
commands many times a second.

42
00:02:26,110 --> 00:02:30,270
Not a wish, not a plan, more like
a very well-rehearsed reflex.

43
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:33,630
But a simulator is never
quite the real world.

44
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:35,370
Real joints have slack.

45
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:37,650
Real floors aren't perfectly flat.

46
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:42,950
Sensors are noisy. And Figure says this
jump had to work at a foundry the team had

47
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:47,370
never visited. A common fix is to
make the simulation messy on purpose.

48
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:53,330
Change
the weight, the friction, the timing a little every round so the policy can't

49
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:55,090
depend on one perfect world.

50
00:02:55,670 --> 00:02:57,630
We don't know which
variations Figure used.

51
00:02:57,990 --> 00:02:59,290
That's just the general idea.

52
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:04,510
Figure
also rehearsed for real, with robots jumping from a second floor onto giant

53
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:07,090
airbags. And the foundry did get messy.

54
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:11,510
Figure says the heat and electromagnetic
noise knocked out cameras and other

55
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:14,130
electronics, and the
policies kept running anyway.

56
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:16,420
So what does this prove?

57
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:21,800
Less
than it looks like. One movement practiced for one kind of target with people

58
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:23,320
arranging everything around it.

59
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:27,900
The steel plant says experts supervised
the work alongside normal production.

60
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:32,460
We haven't seen a failure rate, a test
report, or an independent check of how

61
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:33,920
autonomous each jump was.

62
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:37,320
It shows a trained jump can
survive a brutal environment.

63
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:41,360
It doesn't show a robot that can walk
into any factory and work things out.

64
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:44,590
So why is it so uncomfortable to watch?

65
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:46,510
Partly, it's the shape.

66
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:51,790
When something with arms, legs, and a head
walks to the edge and leaps, it's easy to

67
00:03:51,830 --> 00:03:53,090
see someone making a choice.

68
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:55,310
But the choice wasn't the robot's.

69
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:58,930
People picked the ending, built
the target, and wrote the score.

70
00:03:59,450 --> 00:04:01,570
The policy just got very
good at reaching it.

71
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:04,490
They trained robots to destroy themselves.

72
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:06,350
The robots didn't want anything.

73
00:04:06,710 --> 00:04:07,790
They just stuck the landing.

74
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:12,100
Does retiring hardware feel different
when the hardware looks like us?

